// A derived type may not widen the hierarchy's public interface (decision A).
//
// `Exposer` is the leak this closes: `Base` deliberately keeps `secretHook` protected — an internal seam
// for subclasses — and the subclass republishes it under a public name. Every handle to a `Base` promised
// a fixed surface, and this hands the world a hook the base kept internal. Substitutability has to be
// total, not aspirational: what a base handle can do is what ANY subclass can do, and no more.
//
// A derived type may still add fields, add PRIVATE helpers, and override the protected seams the base
// sanctioned. Constructors are exempt — construction is not part of the substitutable surface.
type virtual resource Base
{
    public ctor make() { }
    public fn int32 rank() { return this.secretHook(); }
    protected virtual fn int32 secretHook() { return 1; }
}
type final resource Exposer extends Base
{
    public ctor make() { this.base = Base.make(); }   // fine — ctors are exempt
    protected override fn int32 secretHook() { return 2; }
    public fn int32 hook() { return this.secretHook(); }       // ERROR: republishes a protected seam
}
fn int main() { Exposer e = Exposer.make(); return e.hook(); }
